@bieniu I think we should 🤔. 

If None is an expected value to be returned, then we can just return it without logging. Else, each value type should be accounted for. Either to be parsed accordingly or to acknowledge that it's an expected type. But IMO, `None` should not be expected.

IIRC @rytilahti once told me that `None` is returned when the device does not support a specific feature. E.g. a vacuum that does not have a mop, and you ask it for its mop attachment status. In this case, it needs to be fixed upstream to not fetch that info at all as it does not even support it.

@frenck what vacuum model are you using? It seems that those features are not supported.

@dshokouhi Does your air purifier have a filter of some sort? I'm no expert on air purifiers, but my educated guess here would be the same. Something is being requested that is not supported.
